WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.700
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, Episode 82 for June 24th, 2026.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:10.220
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:14.860
Let's go. Here's something that's going to change how robots learn to move like us.

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:22.160
Scientists at MIT just found a way to make robot hands copy human gestures by using sound waves instead

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.500
of cameras. Picture this, like trying to teach someone to play piano while wearing thick gloves.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:32.440
You can see their fingers on the outside but you miss all the tiny muscle shifts happening

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:38.920
underneath. The team used ultrasound scans, the same sound wave method doctors rely on to check

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:45.380
on babies before birth. This gives the robot a much clearer map of tendons and muscles in real time.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:50.900
Think of it like switching from watching a concert from the balcony to standing right next to

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:56.160
the musicians and feeling every note. Robot hands have always struggled with delicate work

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:02.840
because cameras only catch the surface. With ultrasound the system hears the moving parts inside

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:09.300
so it can copy motions far more precisely. This matters for future tasks like helping in surgery

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:15.400
or assisting people with daily activities at home. Students who enjoy robotics kits or building

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:20.700
projects might see more affordable helpers appear in coming years because of ideas like this one.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:27.180
You cannot try the robot hand yourself yet but the research video shows the system in action on

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:33.560
the MIT Technology Review site. Search there for the ultrasound robot hand story to watch the clips.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:39.340
Okay now for my favorite part of the show the deep dive where we look under the hood.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:45.740
The idea here is teaching an AI to predict hand movements the way your phone predicts the next word

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:51.960
while you text. Step one uses the fact that ultrasound already works like echoes bouncing around a

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:58.020
cave to create pictures inside the body. Step two attaches a small probe to a person's wrist and

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:04.300
records the inside view during different gestures. Step three feeds those moving images into an AI

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:10.860
so it spots the hidden patterns of muscles and tendons. Step four has the AI direct a robot

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:16.780
hands motors to match the exact motion it just learned and that is basically how the ultrasound

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:22.960
system helps the robot hand become more skillful. So next time someone mentions sensing under the

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:29.320
skin you can explain it is using sound echoes the same way your phone guesses words except it is

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:35.420
guessing movement instead. Not so scary right? Now let's look at two other things worth checking out

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:41.760
this week. Open AI is holding its yearly developer event in San Francisco on September 29.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.980
Anyone can apply to a 10 or watch the keynote online and the deadline is July 10.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:53.320
These events often preview tools that later show up in products like chat GPT.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.800
Go to the developer event application page and read through the kinds of projects people are building.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.560
Even if you are a student it gives a window into what is coming next without needing any coding

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:11.420
skills. Meta's oversight board just gave advice on handling sexualized AI generated images of real

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:18.340
people. Deep fakes are fake photos or videos created by AI that can look completely real and are

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:24.440
sometimes used to harass others online. The suggestions focus on making reports easier and

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:30.160
removing harmful images faster on platforms like Instagram and Facebook. You can read the full

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:34.580
list of recommendations on the Engaget site to see what changes might arrive soon.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:39.620
This kind of update helps keep social media safer for everyone who uses a phone.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:46.200
In quick bits this week people on Reddit are noticing that AI agents can now handle repetitive tasks.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:52.240
Those same agents remember what happened earlier in a conversation. The agents free up time for

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:58.240
more creative work once the repetitive parts are done. Users describe the experience as having a

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:04.220
helpful study buddy. The buddy never gets tired of organizing your notes. The observations appear

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:10.080
in a thread on the chat GPT subreddit. The source link is reddit.com under the post titled

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:16.740
appreciating AI agents. ByteDance, the company behind TikTok showed off a new video tool called

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:24.820
Cdance 2.5. The tool can generate AI clips longer than 30 seconds. Most current tools stop at shorter

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:31.420
lengths. Longer clips open the door for students and creators. They can make short stories or animations.

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:38.520
No expense of equipment is required for these projects. The announcement was covered on the decoder.com.

